  "evidence":[
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v2-1215-joint-delegation","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v2-nesin-tysyatskie","anchor":{"section":"с. 50","record_id":"yakun-and-gyurgiy-invite-yaroslav"},"note":"Nesin’s source discussion reports that in 1215 tysyatsky Yakun Namnezhich and posadnik Gyurgiy Ivanovich went from Novgorod to Pereiaslavl to invite Yaroslav Vsevolodovich. This is a dated joint mission, not a permanent diplomatic competence or present delegation.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v2-1218-princely-distinction","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v2-nesin-tysyatskie","anchor":{"section":"с. 52","record_id":"princely-and-novgorodian-tysyatsky-distinguished"},"note":"Nesin discusses the 1218/1219 chronicle wording that a prince sent ‘his tysyatsky’ and argues the chronicle distinguishes that representative from Novgorod officeholders. It supports a bounded distinction in that political episode, not a universal status or competence rule.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v2-1229-veche-removal","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v2-nesin-tysyatskie","anchor":{"section":"с. 53–54","record_id":"whole-city-veche-removes-vyacheslav"},"note":"Nesin cites the 1228/1229 chronicle: the whole city came from veche, plundered and deprived tysyatsky Vyacheslav, then Boris Negocevich received the office. It is a violent dated succession, not a peaceful or regular election procedure.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v2-1230-hundreds","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v2-nesin-tysyatskie","anchor":{"section":"с. 54","record_id":"1230-property-divided-by-hundreds"},"note":"Nesin cites the 1230 chronicle that after officeholders were removed and replacements selected, Vodovik’s property was divided ‘po stom’. This preserves the source’s hundred terminology in one crisis/property episode; it does not establish standardized territorial hundreds, their officers, jurisdiction, or an administrative map.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v2-mixed-court","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v2-yanin-posadniki","anchor":{"section":"с. 178","record_id":"princely-priority-and-posadnik-in-local-court"},"note":"Yanin’s discussion of mid-XII-century birch-bark material says a posadnik appears as a second addressee in local-court material, while the prince formally had priority, sealed judicial acts and received a seal fee. This is an earlier, source-mediated comparator, not a claim about a 1230 court’s composition, availability, procedure, proof, or remedy.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:residual-government-law-v2-archbishop-title-limit","source_ref":"source:residual-government-law-v2-yanin-posadniki","anchor":{"section":"с. 192","record_id":"bishop-archbishop-title-variation"},"note":"Yanin notes title variation in Novgorod sources after 1165 and warns that it cannot serve as a simple chronological indicator. This is a title-reading limit, not an ecclesiastical competence or a current cleric.","review_status":"approved"}
